      "id": 1,
      "text": "Corydon was founded in 1808 and served as the Indiana Territory capital from 1813 to 1816.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corydon%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Delegates drafted the 1816 constitution in an unfinished log cabin near the courthouse, often taking refuge under the Constitution Elm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corydon%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Corydon Capitol State Historic Site preserves the 1816 courthouse, which opened as a memorial in 1929‑30.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corydon%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "An 8‑mile spur of the Southern Railway linked Corydon to Louisville, New Albany, and other towns beginning in 1882.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corydon%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every summer, the town hosts a reenactment of the Battle of Corydon, a key conflict of the War of 1812.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corydon%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Corydon’s name comes from a hymn called “The Pastoral Elegy” that celebrates a shepherd named Corydon.",
